<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Costa Rica</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1848 CE · capital San José</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Southernmost member of the Central American Federation, a small, relatively stable state where coffee exports began to shape a distinctive society.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1824 after rivalry among its towns over joining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> had been settled by a brief civil war in 1823, after which the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartago-costa-rica">Cartago</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-costa-rica">San José</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-mora-fernandez">Juan Mora Fernández</a> became the first head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Remote from the federal conflicts, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> developed quietly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-mora-fernandez">Mora Fernández</a>, who ruled from 1824 to 1833. Small and medium farmers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-valley-costa-rica">Central Valley</a> took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, which was exported via <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> and, from the 1840s, directly to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and the state encouraged planting with land grants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braulio-carrillo">Braulio Carrillo</a>, head of state from 1835, centralised power, defeated the rival towns in the War of the League and issued a decree of basic guarantees in 1841 that made him ruler for life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> left the federation in November 1838 but kept the title of state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braulio-carrillo">Carrillo</a> was overthrown in 1842 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>, who was himself executed months later. On 31 August 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-castro-madriz">José María Castro Madriz</a> proclaimed the Republic of Costa Rica.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-costa-rica.jpg" alt="State of Costa Rica" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Costa_Rica_(1842%E2%80%931848).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San José</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Mora Fernández, Braulio Carrillo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Free State of Costa Rica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=state-of-costa-rica" aria-label="State of Costa Rica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2361 1807 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Costa Rica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2681 2063L2525 1889M2681 2063L2681 2063"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2525 1889h0M2681 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2681 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2681 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San José. <a href="/atlas?year=1836&amp;focus=state-of-costa-rica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
